Z||000002||Syrin||12-11-2001||03:29 PM||||do i have to take out the engine? fix it from the top or bottom?<P>Could i sell it for 1200?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Syrin||12-11-2001||04:24 PM||||for ONCE could somebody help me dammit, that piece of ****  car is all i own and i need to fix it so PLEASE help.<P>How do i fix the waterpump and how long will it take?||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||12-11-2001||04:25 PM||||All you need are a few hand tools and a few hours of patience.  The pump is cheap for a parts store brand.<BR>Jack it up and remove the right rear wheel and inner fender liner for the easiest access.  Remove the battery for top-down access.  I do both, although some people with an extra elbow and wrist manage to do it from one or the other side only.<BR>Get a Haynes or Chilton's manual to help guide you through it.  The $12 is worth it.<P>Or you could just give up and be Fiero-less.  The choice is yours.  Either way, welcome to the forum.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnF||12-11-2001||04:37 PM||||First lesson in auto buying, huh?<P>Never buy a 16 y.o. piece of transportation for about $1200 and expect perfection all the time.<P>You could have spent over 20 grand it it be in the shop for a water pump.<P>You have bought an older car (a rather rare semi-sports car, at that) for a cheap price.  You must expect to constantly work on and spend on this vehicle just as we a all have done and continue to do.<P>If you decide you can find a better deal for primary transportation, dump your Fiero to someone who wants one and is willing to maintain it.<P>If you keep it, fix it up and tell all of us how great it feels being the proud owner of America's only production mid-engined car.<P>And oh!, BTW - Welcome to the forum. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001107&p=57211671&Sequence=0"> <BR>You can have all the SPEED you can afford!||||reg||12
Z||000006||Syrin||12-11-2001||05:05 PM||||sorry to be rude guys, but this car is all i have, i have hardly any money, no job, i got the car to get a job and ive had it for 3 days and the kid b4 me said hes had no problems for a year straight, i dont have the money to buy a newer car either nor the credit cus of my dads screw ups im also screwed in a couple ways, i would work on this car ALOT if i had a garage but i dont, just a driveway full of snow....||||reg||1
Z||000007||REDHOTT88||12-11-2001||05:22 PM||||Just relax... we are all in this together.<P>I know you are frustrated, but try not to take it out on everyone here. <P>Most everyone has had a problem or another with their Fiero and with lifes little "bumps".<P>Think of your Fiero as Hobby and not just transportation. After a while you'll WANT to replace parts, cause you'll want them new and improved!<P>BTW I just changed the oil today in my driveway, in the snow.. I have no garage.<P>You don't need a garage to work on a car just ambition!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||12-11-2001||06:21 PM||||Took me 6.5 Half-ass hours. It's not a hard job by ANY stretch of the imagination. It *IS* slow work, though, because you don't have that much room. Most of the time is spent trying to get the ratchet onto the bolts. After that, it's just a case of getting them all out, pulling the pump off, blading the old gasket on, fitting the new gasket (and with silicone) and replacing. You WILL lose some skin off your knuckles, but it isn't a hard job. Just be careful not to over-tighten, or break any of the bolts  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I found that dissassembling the battery box and removing the battery made a lot of difference. Truth be told, re-tensioning the belts was the HARDEST part  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000009||Syrin||12-11-2001||06:40 PM||||ok, im also wondering how do i flush the radiatorand get all the crap outta it?<P>cus this kid replaced the engine block and he didnt flush the radiator so theres oil and crap inthere and its nasty, and im sureits not from the engine in there now cus the oil in there looks perfect and it runs nice and fast, but anyway how do i get all that oil n crap outta there?<P>Most of the water/oily stuff is in the overheat container in the front (i forget the name for it) but most of it is in there||||reg||1
Z||000010||avengador1||12-11-2001||06:50 PM||||You could siphon the stuff out of the radiator overflow reservoir. You will have to top of your cooling system after you replace the water pump. Look in Ogre's cave for the burping procedure to eliminate trapped air in the system. Take your time and take a lot of breaks to keep warm. Spray all the bolts with a good penetrating oil to aid in their removal. Most important don't forget where the bolts go that you take out. You could take a piece of cardboard and mount them into it as you remove them. Just out of curiosity, what makes you think that the waterpump is gone? Hope this helps. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Syrin||12-11-2001||07:06 PM||||I heard waterpumps have plugs and when they go they shoot the plug and water sprays out from the hole, and thats what mine is doin, when i start my carafter i put water in it, it sprays it all out||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroBUZZ||12-11-2001||07:37 PM||||When you buy your replacement pump make sure it has a metal impeller rather than plastic. I have found most reman pumps from the parts store are metal. Check it when you buy...<BR>I have heard that the plastic pumps do not hold up well in the Fiero engine and the impeller loosens on the shaft, resulting in pump failure.<BR>Gary<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ayrow||12-12-2001||02:11 AM||||I replaced mine on my 86GT about 8 years ago and it still remains in my mind as one of the most pain-in-ass things I have had to repair on a Fiero.  Took about 6 hours of cussing and guessing as to whether or not your socket wrench is actually grapping the damn bolts.<P>Anyway, it can be done.  Once you have done it once, the second time can be accomplished in about half the time.....not that you want to do it twice.  Howeverm I had no choice since I bought a faulty "new" waterpump from Autozone and had to go in again.<P>Ayrow<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||mblackwell||12-12-2001||04:42 AM||||It is held on by both metric and torx bolts.  I stipped a couple of the torx ones and had to get them out with vice grips.  It took me 5 hours a day for 4 days to get that damn thing replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||12-12-2001||07:57 AM||||when the previous owner said he hadnt fixed anything on it for a year, he meant he kept letting stuff go for a year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Its not that hard unless bolts break. about a 3 hour job for amature. Like above, work thru rear wheel opening, pull the pump out the top. Make sure you use sealer on the bolt threads or they WILL leak. The night before you do it, spray lots of WD40 or liquid wrench on all the bolts so they soak all nite. (i didnt do that, and had a bolt break  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">.. )<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||tesmith66||12-12-2001||08:14 AM||||Be sure to put the gasket on DRY. If you use any sealer, it could hold the gasket away from the block and water will leak out of any voids in the sealer. Do use sealer on the bolt threads (a dab of silicone or some Loc-Tite), and don't forget where everything goes. Be careful with the 6mm bolts, as they thread into the aluminum timing cover. Strip those and you're in for a LOT more work.<P>Take lots of pictures before you tear it apart. There are a lot of parts down there...<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000017||Bill Osmer||12-12-2001||01:13 PM||||I just changed mine (again, the 1st time was 2 years ago) a couple weeks ago. It took about 3 hours, with all of the above (except I did it all from the top with the battery removed). The best techniques for remembering where all the bolts go is to use the replacement box for a template, drawing the outline on the box, punching holes where the bolts are removed from. Then the reassembly will be easier. I also used antisieze (for the next time). I have heard that the aftermarket pumps don't last, so this time I got one from Pontiac. The job is quite do-able by 1st-timers, but just have patience.<P>Don't forget to look up the well-known tecvhniques for for adding coolant after the job is done!!<P>------------------<BR>Bill Osmer<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumcardsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000018||Mach10||12-12-2001||01:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tesmith66:<BR><B>Be sure to put the gasket on DRY. If you use any sealer, it could hold the gasket away from the block and water will leak out of any voids in the sealer. Do use sealer on the bolt threads (a dab of silicone or some Loc-Tite), and don't forget where everything goes. Be careful with the 6mm bolts, as they thread into the aluminum timing cover. Strip those and you're in for a LOT more work.<P>Take lots of pictures before you tear it apart. There are a lot of parts down there...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just follow the gasket directions that were written on the insert. Mine said to use  silicone bead on both sides of the gasket. So I did. Not a single leak.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000019||rogergarrison||12-12-2001||02:53 PM||||Me and a local GM service manager intalled mine. Used OEM and followed the factory service manual. It says a 1/4 inch bead of silicone on gasket surface and to make SURE to use sealant on at least the bottom bolt, which goes rite thru water jacket. Mine dont leak either. I do know another guy with a kitcar like mine, did it without sealer and had to take it out and do all over again, water poured out.||||reg||1
